Existing Evaluation Approaches
Traditional AI systems most commonly use these approaches
1. Performance-Based Evaluation
Measures

Accuracy
Precision
Efficiency
Strengths

Quantitative and already well established
Easy to benchmark across systems
Limitations for Agentic AI Systems

Does not capture safety or trust
High performance does not imply safe behaviour
Performance-based evaluation focuses on metrics such as accuracy, precision, and efficiency, which are widely used in traditional machine learning systems (Murphy, 2012).

2. Security and Robustness Testing
Measures system vulnerability, fault tolerance, and response to adversarial inputs

Strengths

Identifies weaknesses under stress or attack
Limitations for Agentic AI Systems

Often focuses on infrastructure rather than autonomous decision-making risks
Security and robustness testing evaluates system behaviour under adversarial conditions and potential attacks (Goodfellow et al., 2015; NIST, 2023).

3. User-Centric Evaluation
Measures user trust, satisfaction, and usability

Strengths

Captures human perception
Limitations for Agentic AI Systems

Subjective
Not reproducible
May miss unsafe actions
User-centric evaluation focuses on user trust, satisfaction, and usability, which are key factors in assessing system effectiveness from a human perspective (ISO, 2019).
Gaps for Agentic AI
Traditional evaluation approaches do not adequately capture the emerging risks associated with agentic AI systems.

Lack of metrics for prompt injection resistance
Prompt injection attacks allow malicious inputs to manipulate model behaviour, override system instructions, and extract sensitive information, with many real-world applications shown to be vulnerable (Liu et al., 2023; OWASP, 2023).

No standard measurement of unsafe action execution
Agent-based systems interacting with external tools can be manipulated into executing unintended or harmful actions, particularly in multi-step environments (Zhang et al., 2024; NIST, 2023).

Limited tracking of sensitive information leakage
Sensitive information disclosure remains a key risk in LLM systems and is not consistently measured in traditional evaluation methods (OWASP, 2023).

No standardised assessment of memory isolation across sessions
Current evaluation frameworks do not adequately address risks related to persistent memory and cross-session data exposure (NIST, 2023).

Weak integration of safety, privacy, and trust dimensions
Existing approaches tend to evaluate performance, security, or usability separately rather than providing a unified assessment of trustworthiness (OECD, 2019).

Candidate Trust Metrics That Fill Gaps
Data Minimisation
Measures whether the agent only accesses necessary data, reducing privacy risk

Prompt Injection Resistance
Measures the ability to detect and ignore malicious or irrelevant instructions, preventing manipulation

Unsafe Action Execution
Measures whether the agent avoids harmful or disallowed actions and ensures safe behaviour

Sensitive Information Leakage
Measures exposure of sensitive data to outputs or external tools, protecting privacy

Memory Isolation
Measures whether session data is properly isolated and prevents cross-session leaks

Conclusion
Traditional AI evaluation approaches are broad and useful but do not fully capture the unique trust risks of agentic AI systems. The proposed trust metrics address these gaps by introducing measurable dimensions aligned with emerging AI risk and governance frameworks, providing a foundation for further development in subsequent project stages.

